course: everyday
lesson: 348
title: Staying up, screens and coming home late
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Isla in Phoenix kept playing on the tablet after Mum said «screens off». For a whole week nobody noticed, and she began to think nobody ever would. Then Mum saw the glow under her door. No tablet for three days. Isla slammed her door, shouted «It's not fair!» and didn't speak to Mum all evening.
explain: By breakfast the sulk had faded. Mum explained that the three days were a consequence to help her sleep, not revenge, and Isla said sorry. After that she turned it off the first time she was asked — and found she wasn't so tired at school.
explain: Coming home late is worse than it feels. Isla lost track of time at her friend's house and came home an hour late. Mum had been phoning everyone, really frightened. Isla had to come home early for two weeks, said sorry, and now sets an alarm on her watch.
explain: Many rules that feel annoying are about safety and sleep: waiting for the green man, not going to the park alone, coming in when told, knocking before going into a neighbour's garden. A car beeping at you or a bump on the head from the sofa teaches the same lesson the hard way.
fact: Children need plenty of sleep (about 9 to 12 hours a night at this age) to learn and grow.
fact: Screens at bedtime can make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: Getting away with breaking a rule for a while doesn't mean it is safe or fine.
fact: When a child doesn't come home on time, parents can become very frightened.
fact: Telling your parents where you are going keeps you safe.
fact: Crossing at the green signal and waiting for it keeps you safe from cars.
fact: Jumping on furniture can lead to falls and head bumps.
fact: Climbing a tall tree on a dare can leave you stuck or hurt.
fact: A consequence like losing a tablet for a few days is meant to help, not to take revenge.
fact: Going into a neighbour's garden without asking is entering their property; knock and ask first.
exercise: Why did Isla keep playing after «screens off»? | She wanted to finish her game | She didn't hear Mum | The tablet needed charging | Mum had said she could
exercise: Isla got away with it for a week. What did that make her think? | That nobody would ever notice | That screens are good for sleep | That Mum didn't mind at all, really | That the bedtime rule had changed
exercise: How was Isla found out? | Mum saw the glow under her door | The tablet told on her in the morning | Her brother heard the game | The school rang Mum
exercise: Why did Isla slam her door? | She felt the consequence was unfair | She was playing a game and lost it | The door was stuck | Mum asked her to
exercise: What did Mum say the three days without the tablet were? | A consequence to help her sleep, not revenge | A punishment because Mum was cross and tired that night | A way to use the tablet herself | A mistake
exercise: A boy reads under the covers with a torch till late. What happens next day? | He falls asleep in class | He reads faster in the dark | Nothing at all | He gets a prize
exercise: Why do parents worry when a child goes to the park alone without telling them? | They don't know where she is if something goes wrong | They want the park for themselves and their friends after dark | Parks are closed to children | They don't like parks
exercise: Isla came home an hour late. What was Mum doing? | Phoning everyone, frightened | Watching TV, not worried at all | Cooking a big dinner for everyone | Out looking at a new house to buy
exercise: How did Isla make sure she wasn't late again? | Set an alarm on her watch | Stopped visiting friends at all | Asked her friend to remind her | Never looked at the time again
exercise: A boy crosses a big road without waiting for the green man. A car beeps. What is the lesson? | Road rules are there to keep you alive | Cars always stop for children on the crossing | It's fine if you run | It's fine if you get across
exercise: A boy was told three times to stop jumping on the sofa, then fell and banged his head. What does being told three times mean? | The rule matters | Grown-ups like repeating things | It was all a joke | The fourth time it's allowed
exercise: A girl climbs a tall tree on a dare and gets stuck. What would have been wiser? | Say no to the dare and climb only what she's allowed | Climb faster, so she is at the top before she gets scared | Climb with her eyes closed | Climb at night
exercise: A boy eats sweets before dinner when told to wait. What happens? | He isn't hungry for his dinner | He eats more dinner and grows | Nothing happens | He grows faster
exercise: A boy's ball goes into the neighbour's garden. What is the right thing to do? | Knock and ask the neighbour | Climb over quickly | Leave it there forever | Send the dog to get it
exercise: Which helps most after a row about a consequence? | Calming down, saying sorry and talking | Not speaking for a week | Breaking the rule again | Asking the other parent behind their back
test: «been on my tablet after lights out every night, never caught, so it's fine». What's true? | It still hurts her sleep, and she can be caught any night | It's fine if Mum doesn't know, and she's never been caught yet | Tablets help you sleep | Only one hour a night is fine
test: Mum's text: «WHERE ARE YOU. you were meant to be home at 5. ring me NOW». What should Isla do? | Ring at once, say where she is and come home | Wait till she gets home to explain it all properly | Switch the phone off | Text «chill»
test: Isla, door slammed: «you're SO unfair, I hate this house!» What helps most later? | Calm down, say sorry and talk with Mum | Stay in her room for days until Mum gives in | Use the tablet secretly | Shout louder next time
test: Mate: «green man's taking ages, just leg it». Cars are coming. What's wise? | Wait for the green man | Run fast across the road | Go halfway and wait in the middle | Follow the mate wherever he goes
test: Dad: «How many times have I told you? Off the sofa!» It's the third time. What's right? | Get down and play outside instead | Jump once more | Say «in a minute» | Say the sofa is fine
test: Older kids: «climb to the top, scaredy cat». What is the wise answer? | «No thanks» — and stay on the small tree | Climb if they hold the bottom of the trunk | Climb halfway | Climb when they leave
test: Teacher to Mum: «She fell asleep in class again». The girl reads with a torch till late. What's the fix? | Keep to bedtime and read the ending at the weekend | Use a brighter torch so Mum doesn't see the glow | Sleep in class every day | Read faster
test: «went to the park on my own twice, Mum never knew, so it's fine». What's true? | Mum needs to know where she is; not knowing isn't fine | It's fine if she was safe, nothing happened in the end | Twice is fine, three is not | It's fine on weekends
test: Neighbour, stern: «I found you in my garden. Next time knock». What should the boy say? | «Sorry, I'll knock next time» | «It was my ball» | «The gate was open» | «You weren't in»
test: Which is TRUE about bedtime rules? | They help children get the sleep they need to learn | Children need less sleep than adults, so they're for the grown-ups | They're just for grown-ups' quiet | They don't matter on school nights
test: «sweets now, dinner's ages away». Mum said wait. What's wise? | Wait and eat dinner first | Eat a few quickly | Hide them in your sleeve | Eat them and skip dinner
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of a screen after the hour appointed for its cessation. | It may impair sleep, and its non-detection does not render it acceptable | It is acceptable where undetected, since the parent suffers no inconvenience thereby | It improves the quality of sleep | It has no bearing upon sleep
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child ought to inform his parents of his whereabouts. | It enables them to ensure his safety and to find him if required | It is a mere formality without purpose | It enables the parents to restrict his friendships | It is required solely during school hours
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the temporary withdrawal of a tablet following disobedience? | It is a consequence intended to assist the child and not an act of revenge | It relieves the child of any obligation to apologise, the penalty having been paid in full | It is an act of revenge by the parent | It is an arbitrary measure without purpose
exam: Determine the conduct appropriate to a pedestrian who wishes to cross a road at a controlled crossing. | He ought to wait until the green signal is displayed | He ought to cross whenever no vehicle is visible nearby | He ought to cross at a run irrespective of the signal | He ought to follow any companion who crosses
